WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.621 --> 00:00:03.501
(whooshing)
(basketball bounces)

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:05.173
- Passion.
- Passion.

00:00:05.173 --> 00:00:06.631
- Passionate.
- Passion.

00:00:06.631 --> 00:00:08.849
- Passion.
- Hello Believe Nation.

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:11.065
Today we're going to talk
about how you can have passion.

00:00:11.065 --> 00:00:13.073
And as always, guys, as
you're watching, if you hear

00:00:13.073 --> 00:00:15.290
something that really
inspires you, please leave it

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.382
down in the comments below,
put quotes around it as well

00:00:17.382 --> 00:00:20.304
so other people can inspired
and when you write it down,

00:00:20.304 --> 00:00:24.602
it's much more likely to sink
into yourself as well, enjoy.

00:00:24.602 --> 00:00:27.185
(upbeat music)

00:00:35.957 --> 00:00:40.471
- I think the most
beautiful part about life is

00:00:40.471 --> 00:00:43.855
finding what you love to do
and finding your place in it.

00:00:43.855 --> 00:00:46.691
And finding a way to make that a reality,

00:00:46.691 --> 00:00:50.914
whether you love to paint
or to make music or to play

00:00:50.914 --> 00:00:55.674
sports or ya know, write,
whatever it is that you love to

00:00:55.674 --> 00:00:59.774
do in life or whatever it
is that you love to live

00:00:59.774 --> 00:01:03.244
or the people you like
to be around and finding

00:01:03.244 --> 00:01:06.164
ways to make that a reality,
like I fell in love with

00:01:06.164 --> 00:01:08.339
hip hop when I was real little.

00:01:08.339 --> 00:01:10.925
And I started writing
raps and making beats.

00:01:10.925 --> 00:01:14.933
And I was like this is what I
love to spend my time doing.

00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:17.279
I want to find a way
to make that a reality

00:01:17.279 --> 00:01:21.623
and get to travel the
world and get to, life the

00:01:21.623 --> 00:01:22.995
way I want to and share that with people.

00:01:22.995 --> 00:01:24.828
- You went to play music
when you quit school,

00:01:24.828 --> 00:01:26.168
you didn't go to become an actor?

00:01:26.168 --> 00:01:28.048
- Well, I had already,
I was already, I started

00:01:28.048 --> 00:01:29.968
playin' the guitar at the age of 12.

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:33.718
I felt a passion for it
that yeah, it was the

00:01:36.252 --> 00:01:40.419
first real passion that I
had and I can safely say

00:01:41.554 --> 00:01:45.447
because I feel and I felt
then that the word ambition

00:01:45.447 --> 00:01:48.707
has become dirty, ya know.
- Yeah.

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:52.225
- Because ambition
somehow implies that the.

00:01:52.225 --> 00:01:54.813
(baby crying)
I know.

00:01:54.813 --> 00:01:57.279
(laughing)

00:01:57.279 --> 00:02:01.718
I know, I know, cry.
(laughing)

00:02:01.718 --> 00:02:03.768
I'll probably be cryin'
in about 10 minutes so.

00:02:03.768 --> 00:02:06.562
(laughing)

00:02:06.562 --> 00:02:08.396
So ambition was a dirty
word to me because it

00:02:08.396 --> 00:02:11.824
felt like you were working for some game

00:02:11.824 --> 00:02:15.741
or material or some status
so I literally don't

00:02:17.421 --> 00:02:22.061
remember going through
puberty because I locked

00:02:22.061 --> 00:02:25.565
myself in a room at the age of 12, 13.

00:02:25.565 --> 00:02:28.625
And just played the guitar,
learned things off records

00:02:28.625 --> 00:02:33.299
and taught myself how to
play and it was my life.

00:02:33.299 --> 00:02:37.810
And it was the only solace,
it was the only sanctuary.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:41.988
It was the only security,
it was my first love.

00:02:41.988 --> 00:02:46.632
- Many people are what
we call managers or CEOs

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:51.142
and today they can spend seven
years in the watch business

00:02:51.142 --> 00:02:54.619
and eventually then they
might move and be a CEO

00:02:54.619 --> 00:02:57.952
in a car business or in a crystal brand.

00:03:01.343 --> 00:03:05.260
These people are clever
and great entrepreneurs

00:03:07.381 --> 00:03:11.016
but they are not necessarily deeply

00:03:11.016 --> 00:03:13.766
connected forever to one passion.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.033
I am connected just to watches.

00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:23.431
I don't, I would never
like to do another job.

00:03:26.243 --> 00:03:27.918
Of course, I have other little passions.

00:03:27.918 --> 00:03:31.918
I love wine, I love cheese,
I love classic cars.

00:03:34.026 --> 00:03:37.859
But I would never transform
them in a business

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:41.968
because my passion is not deep enough.

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:44.051
While watches is my life.

00:03:45.022 --> 00:03:49.189
If I have to die, I want
people to remember two things.

00:03:50.627 --> 00:03:55.363
I want my children and my
wife to remember my love

00:03:55.363 --> 00:03:57.497
and I want people to remember my

00:03:57.497 --> 00:04:00.763
contribution to the watch business.

00:04:00.763 --> 00:04:03.936
So that's really my
objective, that's why I would

00:04:03.936 --> 00:04:07.990
never leave this business and not many.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:12.157
I have been in the business
since '74 so it's nearly

00:04:14.298 --> 00:04:18.465
40 years, 39 years in the
same business and I don't know

00:04:19.871 --> 00:04:24.797
many people in this business
that have been 39 years there.

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:29.147
Some great people came but
they came from a car brand,

00:04:29.147 --> 00:04:32.535
came from Ferrari or from
Maserati and joined the watch

00:04:32.535 --> 00:04:34.416
business and they've been seven years.

00:04:34.416 --> 00:04:37.260
They going to move and
go to Hermes or Veto.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.427
So it's different, I am a
watch fan, a watch freak.

00:04:42.938 --> 00:04:44.478
- People won't know what
I went through between

00:04:44.478 --> 00:04:47.730
the second and third
album, of the fear that was

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.396
implemented in me, even
though I knew I was talented.

00:04:51.396 --> 00:04:54.589
I have to be confident in what I do.

00:04:54.589 --> 00:04:56.799
And that song was one of the
first songs that I recorded

00:04:56.799 --> 00:04:59.143
for the album and I was
actually going to call the album

00:04:59.143 --> 00:05:00.905
Masterpiece and I just thought
no, it doesn't make sense.

00:05:00.905 --> 00:05:02.539
If people hear it on
its own, it just sounds

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:04.755
like I'm bein' an absolute asshole.

00:05:04.755 --> 00:05:07.599
But for me, I've never made it.

00:05:07.599 --> 00:05:08.685
I've never finished my paintin'.

00:05:08.685 --> 00:05:11.899
Until the day that my eyes
close and I don't wake up,

00:05:11.899 --> 00:05:15.076
I feel like I'm still puttin'
towards my masterpiece.

00:05:15.076 --> 00:05:19.243
And I want to work hard and I'm
okay to say, hey, I messed up.

00:05:20.208 --> 00:05:22.246
I do things, I say things,
I wear things, I date people

00:05:22.246 --> 00:05:26.470
I shouldn't, I'm human and
we're all building on something

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:30.637
that we hope can leave a
standing of the test of time.

00:05:31.871 --> 00:05:33.797
And I think that that's
what that song means to me

00:05:33.797 --> 00:05:36.603
and I, I really, really put
it towards the other people

00:05:36.603 --> 00:05:40.155
that have gone, you can't
do this or lied about me

00:05:40.155 --> 00:05:42.665
and said I'm difficult or
this and that and called me a

00:05:42.665 --> 00:05:45.919
diva and when I'm not, I'm passionate.

00:05:45.919 --> 00:05:49.018
No, there's a very, very
thin, when people can't deal

00:05:49.018 --> 00:05:50.986
with how passionate you
are, they attack you.

00:05:50.986 --> 00:05:52.702
And that's the same with anyone.

00:05:52.702 --> 00:05:55.838
- Because everybody kept asking
me, what does it feel like,

00:05:55.838 --> 00:05:58.638
what does it feel like,
when you got the nomination,

00:05:58.638 --> 00:06:00.898
that little girl from
Washington DC, what did

00:06:00.898 --> 00:06:03.947
it feel like when you heard your name?

00:06:03.947 --> 00:06:05.331
- Mine was a roller coaster ride.

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:07.713
I was nominated for this
and wasn't for that.

00:06:07.713 --> 00:06:09.970
So I really put it out
of my head 'cause I don't

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.887
really do what I do for the awards.

00:06:13.818 --> 00:06:16.664
Like you said, if you're
passionate about your work,

00:06:16.664 --> 00:06:18.880
all the accolades will come.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.396
You got to stay focused on what
God put you on Earth to do.

00:06:23.396 --> 00:06:25.276
So that's number one.
(applause)

00:06:25.276 --> 00:06:26.862
- Yeah, I mean, I was very fortunate.

00:06:26.862 --> 00:06:29.362
I started trying to work at 14

00:06:31.077 --> 00:06:34.547
and I got on a sit com when I was 16.

00:06:34.547 --> 00:06:37.343
I dealt with a lot of
no's, mostly personal no's,

00:06:37.343 --> 00:06:39.185
within my family, it's
hard for a 14 year old

00:06:39.185 --> 00:06:42.571
to say, I'm leaving Kentucky,
I'm moving to New York.

00:06:42.571 --> 00:06:45.664
But I knew, I felt so
strongly, it feels insane

00:06:45.664 --> 00:06:49.084
to be a teenager and know,
I knew that it was the

00:06:49.084 --> 00:06:50.754
right thing to do, I knew
it was going to work out.

00:06:50.754 --> 00:06:51.966
- Were they supportive, your family?

00:06:51.966 --> 00:06:54.008
- Well no, I wouldn't be
supportive if my teenager

00:06:54.008 --> 00:06:56.270
was like, I'm movin' to New York.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:59.022
They thought, ya know, they
thought I was going to get killed.

00:06:59.022 --> 00:07:02.776
I'm surprised I didn't get
killed but it was like this

00:07:02.776 --> 00:07:06.109
fire and also this like, I just knew it.

00:07:07.877 --> 00:07:09.931
So I eventually saved up babysitting money

00:07:09.931 --> 00:07:12.019
and went and did it.

00:07:12.019 --> 00:07:15.409
- What about the business
of being what you are now,

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:18.417
the musician, the
songwriter, I mean, did you

00:07:18.417 --> 00:07:21.470
display an early talent for singing?

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:23.894
- I didn't actually, I got
thrown out of the choir.

00:07:23.894 --> 00:07:25.267
- [Interviewer] Did you?
(laughing)

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:27.317
- I was thrown out of
the choir for, I think,

00:07:27.317 --> 00:07:29.699
as far as I know, it was for talking.

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:32.751
And I always remember my
dad used to say to me,

00:07:32.751 --> 00:07:36.918
'cause I used to say, Dad,
that when I was like 16, 17, 18

00:07:37.811 --> 00:07:40.153
and my dad was threatening
to throw me out of the house

00:07:40.153 --> 00:07:42.408
if I didn't get a job within
a certain amount of time

00:07:42.408 --> 00:07:43.830
so obviously I went out
and got jobs but I was

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:46.428
saying, I want to be a singer,
I think I'm going to be

00:07:46.428 --> 00:07:51.290
rich and famous and he would
say, everybody wants to be

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:52.874
a pop singer, everybody wants.

00:07:52.874 --> 00:07:54.624
And I would say no Dad,
everybody wants to be

00:07:54.624 --> 00:07:58.396
a pop singer when they're
12, ya know, when they're 12.

00:07:58.396 --> 00:08:01.911
I've, this is my whole life,
this is what I want to do.

00:08:01.911 --> 00:08:05.213
But I've lost my train of thought.

00:08:05.213 --> 00:08:06.213
(laughing)

00:08:06.213 --> 00:08:08.265
- Was it to be rich and
famous, your ambition,

00:08:08.265 --> 00:08:09.269
I mean, was that it?
- No.

00:08:09.269 --> 00:08:11.191
I think I had two very,
very strong ambitions

00:08:11.191 --> 00:08:14.537
and they've always fought
against one another, always.

00:08:14.537 --> 00:08:18.606
And one was a passion for
music and an ability to

00:08:18.606 --> 00:08:22.528
communicate through music
and really a sense of purpose

00:08:22.528 --> 00:08:26.282
through music and two
was this desire to be

00:08:26.282 --> 00:08:28.702
and rich and famous is not
right because actually,

00:08:28.702 --> 00:08:30.998
rich never came into it,
I actually realized about

00:08:30.998 --> 00:08:33.509
six months ago, someone
put a question to me

00:08:33.509 --> 00:08:37.603
and I actually realized
that at no point during

00:08:37.603 --> 00:08:42.121
my early life when I
was, in all my ambitions

00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:45.681
or even when I started
to realize my ambitions,

00:08:45.681 --> 00:08:48.981
at no point did it ever
occur to me that one of the

00:08:48.981 --> 00:08:51.656
byproducts of this would be
that I could buy whatever I

00:08:51.656 --> 00:08:54.496
wanted or live in a big
house, have a flashy car,

00:08:54.496 --> 00:08:56.586
all the things that are very pleasant.

00:08:56.586 --> 00:08:59.848
But it really hadn't occurred
to me, it never occurred to

00:08:59.848 --> 00:09:01.894
me so I didn't want to be rich, I

00:09:01.894 --> 00:09:04.154
just wanted to be filthily famous.

00:09:04.154 --> 00:09:06.904
(downbeat music)

00:09:16.447 --> 00:09:18.921
(screaming)

00:09:18.921 --> 00:09:21.083
- [Interviewer] What did
Cronos achieve for you?

00:09:21.083 --> 00:09:23.509
- Well, that movie changed
my life because we were

00:09:23.509 --> 00:09:26.514
more than the underdog,
more than the dark horse.

00:09:26.514 --> 00:09:29.818
We were a movie that
nobody wanted to produce,

00:09:29.818 --> 00:09:33.366
nobody was supporting except
my producers in Mexico.

00:09:33.366 --> 00:09:38.052
And we suddenly were selected
for CAN and literally,

00:09:38.052 --> 00:09:41.063
we were all of the sudden
one of the most awarded

00:09:41.063 --> 00:09:43.646
movies in Mexican Film History.

00:09:45.031 --> 00:09:49.198
I remember the first time
Cronos won the first award

00:09:50.093 --> 00:09:54.145
which was a cash award
and before that award,

00:09:54.145 --> 00:09:56.851
we were in debt for half a million.

00:09:56.851 --> 00:10:01.740
We won the award and literally
like a beauty contest,

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:05.832
I was crying, crying in
the stage holding this

00:10:05.832 --> 00:10:10.050
giant check and I was trembling
and I was really moved

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:13.018
because a, I was happy the
movie was being recognized

00:10:13.018 --> 00:10:16.114
and b, I was not going to jail.

00:10:16.114 --> 00:10:20.843
- I watched so many Metros
in my life, unbelievable

00:10:20.843 --> 00:10:25.010
and really not so, some
boring games and then I sleep.

00:10:26.867 --> 00:10:30.507
And it's so boring, why
they beat each other

00:10:30.507 --> 00:10:34.398
and make this 20, 40,
50, 60, 70, 80,000 people

00:10:34.398 --> 00:10:37.330
in a stadium with this
boring game, it's not okay.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:40.590
So that's what we want to
see, we want to enjoy our own

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:44.062
game, if we lose, okay, we like to win.

00:10:44.062 --> 00:10:46.738
But if we lose with our way, it's okay.

00:10:46.738 --> 00:10:48.578
If we lose with the way of somebody else,

00:10:48.578 --> 00:10:52.328
it's, you cannot work
with this so that's it.

00:10:53.171 --> 00:10:56.838
It's very emotional,
very fast, very strong,

00:10:58.657 --> 00:11:01.324
not boring, no chess, like this.

00:11:02.207 --> 00:11:05.874
Yeah, of course, tactical
but with big heart

00:11:07.955 --> 00:11:11.307
and tactical things are so important.

00:11:11.307 --> 00:11:13.227
You cannot win without tactical things

00:11:13.227 --> 00:11:16.227
but the emotion make the difference.

00:11:17.063 --> 00:11:20.053
Life in our game, that's important.

00:11:20.053 --> 00:11:21.519
- Well, I've always played the violin.

00:11:21.519 --> 00:11:23.903
Ever since I was six years
old, I begged for lessons.

00:11:23.903 --> 00:11:26.910
And I've always played and
it was actually when I was in

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:29.584
college that I kind of just
realized one day that I didn't

00:11:29.584 --> 00:11:32.924
love it anymore and I used to love it.

00:11:32.924 --> 00:11:35.708
And I specifically didn't
study music in school

00:11:35.708 --> 00:11:38.466
because I didn't ever want
to have the love of my

00:11:38.466 --> 00:11:41.099
music be out of me so it
kind of broke my heart

00:11:41.099 --> 00:11:43.147
when I was like, I don't
even enjoy this anymore.

00:11:43.147 --> 00:11:45.743
And so I started thinking,
okay, what can I do

00:11:45.743 --> 00:11:48.339
to like invigorate my passion,
like I started playing

00:11:48.339 --> 00:11:51.353
with bands, I started just
anywhere I could go to kind of

00:11:51.353 --> 00:11:54.569
try out new styles of
music and I've always loved

00:11:54.569 --> 00:11:57.121
electronic music and
it just hit me one day.

00:11:57.121 --> 00:11:58.794
I need to play and write the kind

00:11:58.794 --> 00:12:01.352
of music that I am loving right now.

00:12:01.352 --> 00:12:04.574
(funky music)

00:12:04.574 --> 00:12:07.622
And so I did and it just
brought it back to life for me.

00:12:07.622 --> 00:12:09.710
It made it so creative
and I became a creator,

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.426
not just a musician and
that's, I think, what

00:12:11.426 --> 00:12:14.267
just invigorated my passion.

00:12:14.267 --> 00:12:15.939
- So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:12:15.939 --> 00:12:17.823
I'd love to know what did
you think of this video.

00:12:17.823 --> 00:12:20.293
What clip resonated the
most with you that you're

00:12:20.293 --> 00:12:22.639
going to take and immediately
apply to your life

00:12:22.639 --> 00:12:24.769
or your business somehow,
leave it down in the comments

00:12:24.769 --> 00:12:26.937
below and I'm going to
join in the discussion.

00:12:26.937 --> 00:12:29.341
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Karim Al-Kassem.

00:12:29.341 --> 00:12:31.099
Thank you so much Karim
for picking up a copy of my

00:12:31.099 --> 00:12:33.645
book, Your One Word, it
really, really, really

00:12:33.645 --> 00:12:35.989
means a lot to me and I
hope you're enjoying it.

00:12:35.989 --> 00:12:37.658
So thank you guys, again, for watching.

00:12:37.658 --> 00:12:39.534
I believe in you, I hope
you continue to believe

00:12:39.534 --> 00:12:43.958
in yourself and whatever
your One Word is, much love.

00:12:43.958 --> 00:12:45.296
I'll see you soon.

00:12:45.296 --> 00:12:47.508
(whooshing)
(basketball bounces)

